  *Abstract.* PlanetSimulation couples terrain, prescribed orbital motion, reflected illumination, and an optically active atmosphere in an interactive renderer. This paper explains the physical ideas behind those models and the approximations that make them practical. Particular attention goes to atmospheric density, molecular and aerosol extinction, single scattering, twilight visibility, and refractive ray curvature. For each major subsystem, we compare the implemented method with a plausible alternative and state what physical behavior is retained or lost. Twelve generated vector diagrams explain the algorithms; versioned captures show actual rendering. The reported GPU benchmark comes from the project README and is qualified by its original workload and hardware. The model is intentionally limited: it does not solve climate, fluid flow, multiple atmospheric scattering, or mutual N-body gravity.
]
#v(0.3cm)
*Keywords:* real-time graphics · procedural terrain · Keplerian dynamics · atmospheric optics · adaptive rendering · reproducible engineering

= Scope and physical questions

The paper describes the current source and its reproducible images, not a claim of physical fidelity for every planet. The useful questions are causal: what determines a surface point, what gravitational model moves the bodies, what radiance reaches the eye, and which terms can be approximated without destroying the intended visual behavior? Equations are simplified descriptions of the implementation; where a coefficient is tuned for the miniature scene, that is stated explicitly. Images come from `docs/screenshots/`, with capture inputs and hashes in `docs/captures/generation.json`. Diagrams in `figures/` are explanatory schematics generated by `make_figures.py`.

The atmosphere deserves special care. A convincing sunset depends on the length and density of the optical path, not merely on a sky color. The renderer therefore keeps absorption, out-scattering, in-scattering, and geometric visibility as separate quantities. Its reference pressure and temperature are configured inputs; no weather or climate equation evolves them. Tests and controlled captures check specific invariants and regressions, but an attractive image alone cannot establish that the numerical model is physically accurate.

= Representing a planet in a camera

== From configuration to a projected sphere

A position in a planet's local mesh is transformed by a model matrix, a camera view matrix, and a projection matrix. In the project's column-vector convention,

$ x_"clip" = P V M x_"local", quad x_"ndc" = x_"clip" / w_"clip" . $

This mapping establishes which physical coordinates each calculation uses. Heights belong to the rotating planet frame; orbital positions and light directions belong to the world frame; depth and pixel reconstruction belong to the camera frame. Mixing them can make a mountain move under a stationary observer or make a shadow follow the camera. GLM supplies the matrix operations, while behavior tests check the resulting projections. @fig:transform records the data path.

#figure(image("figures/early/transforms.svg", width: 100%), caption: [The foundational transform and shading path. `P V M` follows the current shader convention; the final display transform happens after linear-light composition. Generated schematic.]) <fig:transform>

A hidden-window `--render-test` and PNG smoke test make camera framing, framebuffer readback, and image output observable in headless environments. @fig:solar is an actual capture of a compact regression scene. It is evidence of the current renderer at a fixed input state.

#figure(image("../screenshots/solar-view.png", width: 82%), caption: [Headless rendering of the compact scenario. It checks the projected positions of the bodies at a reproducible input state. Source: `docs/screenshots/solar-view.png`; provenance: `docs/captures/generation.json`.]) <fig:solar>

== A reproducible scene as an experimental unit

The JSON parser performs type and range checks before GPU resources are replaced. Scene replay stores a resolved scenario, camera, time, and quality choices next to a PNG. The image is an observation; its sidecar specifies a repeatable input state. On live reload, a new CPU scene is staged before the current one is exchanged, so an invalid or half-written file retains the last valid scene. This matters for physical comparison: changing the camera or exposure at the same time as atmospheric density would confound the result.

= Terrain as a sampled physical surface

== Procedural surface as a camera-independent function

The surface height is evaluated in a normalized planet-local radial direction $u$. A simplified form is

$ h(u) = h_"landscape"(u) + sum_i A_i f_i(u; "seed"_i, "frequency"_i, "octaves"_i). $

The implemented functions include value-noise fBm and a ridged variant. Broad continent and cliff fields determine large-scale shape; finer octaves add local detail. This is synthetic topography: it has spatial correlation and multiple length scales but no tectonics, erosion, or material transport. The seed and local coordinate frame make repeated samples of the same point deterministic. Since terrain geometry is rebuilt as the camera moves, a view-dependent height function would make the ground itself rise and fall. The current code samples `heightAt(radial)` during every LOD rebuild. @fig:terrain distinguishes the fixed field from its variable triangulation.

#figure(image("figures/early/terrain.svg", width: 100%), caption: [The height field is fixed in planet coordinates; eight distance levels change density and apply a bounded inward offset to coarser regions. Bars show the working segment anchors and interpolated levels; the sphere is schematic. Generated schematic.]) <fig:terrain>

The sphere begins with 20 icosahedral faces, subdivided twice into 320 stable base faces. For nearby views, each base face is assigned one of eight edge-segment levels using its angular distance to the eye. A previous-zone hysteresis margin prevents repeated switching at a threshold. Steep faces can receive extra segments if the triangle budget permits. Edges shared by unequal zones are sampled once at the finer requirement, then reused on both sides; concentric interior rings fill the faces without T-junctions. The budget loop first removes optional steep refinement and then lowers less-important zones. This preserves one continuous sampled surface under a per-planet triangle ceiling.

The working segment sequence, from coarse to fine, is 3, 5, 6, 8, 10, 12, 14, 16. Existing base, middle and maximum settings anchor levels 0, 3 and 7. Seven surface-distance boundaries span 300 to 1000 m. The three-zone counts in older reports remain an aggregate (level 0, levels 1–6, level 7); captures now also print all eight counts.

Coarse samples receive an inward displacement $s = s_"max" ((n_"max" - n) / (n_"max" - n_"base"))^2$, clamped to zero for extra-steep detail. The configured `sink_depth_m` defaults to 1 m, additionally bounded to 0.1% of the radius remaining after maximum relief. Shared corners use the smallest incident offset; edges use the finer neighbor, with smooth interpolation toward corners and face centers. Shore refinement carries this scalar field through subdivision before displacement is applied. There is exactly one selected surface per direction, so coarse shells cannot poke through a simultaneously rendered fine shell. The sea uses the same eight-level selection with zero sinking. Geometry, shadows, reflections and foliage roots agree on the installed mesh.

This is a spatial sinking rule, not an animated LOD crossfade. Hysteresis and stable choices preserve vertices within unchanged levels; crossing a level can still move geometry. An offset below the sampled height also does not establish an error bound everywhere inside a triangle on arbitrary procedural relief. The accompanying `benchmarks/terrain-lod.md` records tests and controlled captures.

Two fixed before/after views (shoreline and grass) use all eight levels. Each already reaches the 100,000-triangle land budget and 60,000-triangle water budget, so this change redistributes detail rather than reducing those totals. Land uploads remain 12,000,000 bytes per rebuild; the sink scalar adds CPU metadata but no GPU vertex attribute or draw. The shoreline retains 19 finest base faces and the grass view retains 18. Capture timings overlapped compilation and are not used as performance evidence. Replay inputs, image pairs, logs and hashes are retained under `benchmarks/terrain-lod/`.

An alternative is a cube-sphere quadtree with screen-space error bounds. It can allocate triangles more directly to projected error and support very large planets, but needs explicit seam and level-transition rules. A fixed high-resolution sphere avoids LOD transitions at the cost of drawing distant detail that cannot affect the image. Here the icosahedral base and fixed height query suit a small interactive scene where local walking must remain stable.

The sampled height feeds normals and terrain color factors as well as eye clearance. Water is a separate spherical shell, an approximation that omits tides and waves in the body's shape. The local camera follows the planet's translation and rotation through a planet-fixed frame, so the same latitude/longitude refers to the same terrain location after orbital motion or spin. Walking near a pole uses a great-circle step and parallel transport of the view basis; longitude itself becomes singular there. @fig:pole illustrates why transporting the basis in 3-D is better behaved than rebuilding it from longitude after every step.

#figure(image("figures/early/pole.svg", width: 100%), caption: [Great-circle position update and basis transport across the north pole. A longitude-derived frame can flip at the pole; a transported 3-D basis remains continuous. Generated schematic.]) <fig:pole>

#figure(image("../screenshots/surface-view.png", width: 83%), caption: [Surface-camera capture of procedurally generated terrain. Geometry derives from the stable height function while tessellation is selected for this camera. Source: current gallery.]) <fig:surface>

= Orbital motion and light transport

== Prescribed Kepler ellipses with collective mass

A body names its parent and supplies semi-major and semi-minor axes $a,b$, a mean anomaly, inclination, ascending node, and periapsis orientation. The two-body approximation gives eccentricity and period as

$ e = sqrt(1 - b^2 / a^2), quad mu = G (m_"parent" + m_"collective"), quad T = 2 pi sqrt(a^3 / mu). $

Here $a$ and $b$ are converted from the scenario's distance unit to metres before applying the SI gravitational constant; the code performs the equivalent calculation in world units. At time $t$, mean anomaly advances linearly, representing equal areas swept in equal times. A bounded bracketing solve obtains eccentric anomaly $E$ from $M = E - e sin E$, and the orbital-plane position is $(a(cos E-e), b sin E)$. Three rotations orient that ellipse in the world. The implementation uses a rationalized form near extreme periapsis to avoid cancellation. Evaluating at absolute time avoids frame-rate-dependent drift and makes pausing, replay, and speed changes deterministic.

#figure(image("figures/dynamics/kepler.svg", width: 100%), caption: [An ellipse is specified relative to a focus; a child subtree and its parent recoil around a mass-weighted center. The right panel abstracts the Earth–Moon relation; sizes and offsets are not to scale. Generated schematic.]) <fig:kepler>

Each subtree acts as a collective point mass in its parent's prescribed ellipse; children induce mass-weighted recoil of the parent. Thus an Earth–Moon pair has an internal barycenter, and the Sun moves in response to its descendants while its configured position remains the time-zero anchor. This retains the main geometric and mass-weighted effects while omitting inter-branch perturbations and tidal evolution. A symplectic N-body integrator would capture mutual perturbations and long-term exchange of energy and angular momentum, but would need initial velocities, a suitable time step, and careful treatment of close encounters. Prescribed ellipses are a good fit for deterministic scene replay, not for predicting orbital evolution.

For the `O` overlay, the application samples the complete hierarchy at 321 times for each body: ten revolutions at 32 segments per revolution. A moon's inertial trail must inherit its parent's translation rather than drawing one stationary local ellipse. @fig:trails shows that distinction.

#figure(image("figures/dynamics/orbit-trails.svg", width: 100%), caption: [Illustrative moon trail in the inertial world frame. The repeated local cycles travel with the parent; the curve is explanatory and does not plot a measured scenario trajectory. Generated schematic.]) <fig:trails>

== Light transport before atmospheric composition

Per-frame CPU lighting computes direct flux with inverse-square distance and reflected flux with geometric albedo, a diffuse-sphere phase function, and inverse-square reflector-to-receiver falloff. Sun luminosity is derived from absolute bolometric magnitude using the IAU zero point. The phase law matters: a full moon presents more of its illuminated hemisphere to the receiver than a crescent. Shaders sum direct, reflected, and indirect contributions in linear RGB before an exponential display shoulder and sRGB encoding. Summing encoded display values would break the proportional meaning of flux and exposure.

A directional orthographic depth map is made per planet from the local Sun direction. The same map constrains opaque terrain, water, and direct atmospheric scattering. Its depth test approximates geometric occlusion by terrain; it does not solve light diffraction or soft penumbrae from the full solar disk. The map is cached until local Sun direction, terrain revision, extent, or resolution changes enough to alter its result. Ray tracing each point toward the Sun would give a more direct visibility test and potentially better soft shadows, but its cost is high for every surface fragment and atmospheric sample. @fig:lighting separates the direct and reflected paths.

#figure(image("figures/dynamics/lighting.svg", width: 100%), caption: [The Sun contributes direct flux to each body, the Moon reflects a phase-dependent portion toward the planet, and terrain visibility controls direct light. The diagram is schematic; arrow widths do not encode flux.]) <fig:lighting>

