== Interpreting motion from the camera

Ten-revolution predictive trails and body cards help distinguish local orbits from inertial paths. The path color averages terrain vertex RGB factors multiplied by the body's configured base tint; labels report name, parent, axes, eccentricity, period, radius, and mass. Sampling the entire orbital system, rather than each child's local ellipse alone, is necessary for moving-parent trails. The overlay is toggled by `O` and hidden in planet and surface cameras; real X11 input tests verify both behaviors.

Orbit-to-surface entry is a one-second wall-clock transition. The starting pose is the last actually rendered view, avoiding a first-frame jump after the key event. An eased parameter $q(u)=3u^2-2u^3$ drives spherical interpolation of the planet-relative radial direction, logarithmic interpolation of radius, quaternion interpolation of orientation, and field-of-view interpolation. Intermediate positions are clamped above sampled terrain and water. The target is resampled while the planet moves, so the transition ends at the current surface pose. This is a camera path rather than physically accelerated descent. The astronaut flight mode separately integrates forces, velocity and collisions. @fig:transition shows the path and ease curve.

#figure(image("figures/interaction/transition.svg", width: 100%), caption: [Orbit-to-surface descent. The curve shows the implemented smoothstep parameter; the spatial path is schematic because the actual start, target, and terrain vary by scene.]) <fig:transition>

= Astronaut locomotion and flight

Camera 4 follows an original procedural astronaut at the same surface location as camera 2. Normal movement is 6 m/s; either Shift key raises it to 12 m/s. The grey and blue suit carries small German upper-arm patches. Body-local grounded contacts follow planet translation and spin, while the grounded chase camera clears the ground and shortens its arm at intervening terrain. Airborne position and velocity use an inertial world frame.

An alternating gait preserves the stance foot and moves only the swinging foot, using a smooth ground-projected trajectory with 15 cm lift. Contacts intersect the installed terrain triangles, including LOD sinking, and resample height after mesh replacement without changing radial location. The MIT-licensed ozz-animation 0.16.0 runtime supplies two-bone leg IK near the actor origin. This combines procedural step selection with a tested joint solver; it is an initial locomotion approximation rather than a motion-captured running cycle. Once walking settles, the pelvis rises over the locked ankles until both knees extend on level ground. The suit and chase view share this adjustment; uneven ground retains the necessary bend. The 0.10 s settling response preserves segment lengths, and exactly straight chains use a collinear midpoint to avoid an IK singularity. Replay includes the body offset; details and the before/after view are in `character/standing.md`.

Space launches at 10 m/s; holding it supplies upward thrust while airborne. A 100 kg actor integrates world-space gravity and quadratic drag relative to moving air, using configured pressure, gas composition and temperature. The suit tilts to direct its single downward exhaust axis, and arms remain quiet while the jetpack is armed. Falling has no artificial velocity cap; drag determines the local terminal speed. Landing resets the flight state. Flight uses full wall-clock elapsed time with 10 ms substeps and interpolated body motion, while grounded walking retains its presentation-time bound. The coasting exhaust axis remains inertial during planet spin.

The production main planet sizes a shared 8.29 kN engine, retained on other planets. At sea level, 100 m/s produces approximately 4.24 kN drag and 424 kW useful horizontal power. Assuming 1,000 m/s exhaust and 60% conversion efficiency, installed maximum thrust corresponds to about 6.91 MW exhaust input. These are explicit idealised choices, not measurements of a real jetpack. Attainable speed depends on gravity, air density and direction on the rapidly spinning miniature planet; the 100 m/s command changes force rather than clipping velocity. The reproducible calculation and limits are in `character/flight.md`. Fuel and thermal limits remain future work.


Airborne WASD now ignites thrust without Space and follows the full camera direction, including look-down descent; Space commands up. Within centre distance 1.2 diameters (2.4 radii), up follows the nearby planet or moon with a 0.35 s response. Beyond all such regions it remains inertial, with unrestricted space-camera pitch. The astronaut can approach the Moon and adopt its frame without teleporting or resetting velocity. Overlapping regions use 10% centre-distance hysteresis. Each flight step sums gravity from the three nearest celestial centres, including the Sun. Atmospheric drag is independent of the orientation switch. Space thrust requests acceleration without the near-body 100 m/s governor. Full details and world-state replay are in `character/space-flight.md`.

